desc: Veil
// Designed by Rozaya, developed with Claude (Anthropic)
//
// This work is dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal.
// https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
//
//tags: veil womb voice muffle muffled underwater lowpass prenatal fetal in-utero stereo
//
// Muffles a MONO voice the way the womb does — a steep lowpass around
// ~500 Hz, "like speech from behind a heavy curtain." (Real prenatal
// hearing: tissue + fluid attenuate everything above ~600 Hz; researchers
// mimic it with a ~400 Hz lowpass, and newborns actually PREFER that
// filtered version of the mother's voice.)
//
// === Stereo from a mono source ===
//
// A lowpass can't make something stereo — a mono voice stays a point in
// the centre. Width has to be MANUFACTURED by making the two channels
// different. The Left and Right chains are fully independent, each with
// its own cutoff and resonance. The WIDTH is the DIFFERENCE between the
// two cutoffs — pull them apart for more width, together for mono.
// Because the channels differ only in spectral rolloff (no phase/delay
// offset), a mono sum just averages the two rolloffs: no comb-notch
// cancellation, safe on a single speaker / HomePod / phone.
//
// === Slope ===
//
// Each SVF lowpass stage is -12 dB/oct; Slope cascades 1-4 of them for
// -12 / -24 / -36 / -48 dB/oct. Steeper = more muffled. Shared across
// both channels (a discrete character choice, not a drift target).
//
// === Drift + Speed Ramp (per channel) ===
//
// All four per-channel params — both cutoffs AND both resonances — are
// independent Drift and Speed Ramp targets, so the muffle can MOVE:
// a living womb, not a static EQ.
//   * Drift = a slow perpetual wander on a target. Give the two channels
//     slightly different periods and the stereo width itself breathes.
//   * Speed Ramp = a one-time signed ride on a target over N minutes.
//     Positive `by` on both cutoffs = the voice slowly CLEARING (the
//     muffle opening as the baby "grows"); negative = deepening.
// Nested-selector pattern: one target selector + shared up/down/period/
// shape sliders; ALL targets drift in parallel, the selector just picks
// which one you're editing. The amount is in the TARGET'S native unit —
// Hz for cutoff, 0-1 for resonance ("units match target", the suite
// convention; you tune it by ear). Cutoff uses the big end of the amount
// range, resonance the 0-1 end.
//
// Pair with: Womb (heartbeat/breath bed underneath), Bubbler / Dapple
// (fluid texture).

slider1:480<100,2000,1>Left cutoff (Hz)
slider2:520<100,2000,1>Right cutoff (Hz)
slider3:0.15<0,1,0.01>Left resonance
slider4:0.15<0,1,0.01>Right resonance
slider5:0<0,5,1{-12,-24,-36,-48,-60,-72}>Slope (dB/oct)
slider6:0<-24,12,0.01>Output (dB)

// --- Drift (nested selector) ---
slider7:0<0,3,1{Left cutoff,Right cutoff,Left resonance,Right resonance}>Drift target
slider8:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift up amount (units match target)
slider9:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift down amount (units match target)
// Unit is set by slider11 (Drift period unit) — seconds by default, beats in
// Host-synced projects. The unit is deliberately NOT in this label: NVDA
// re-reads the parameter name on every arrow step, and a name that lies half
// the time is worse than one that's short.
slider10:20<1,600,0.1>Drift period
// Host tempo sync for the drift period. Veil has no rate — the only musical
// length in it is the drift period, so that is the only thing Host x touches.
// Seconds is the default; Beats reinterprets slider10 as beats and follows the
// project tempo. GLOBAL, not per target: one drift clock unit for the plugin.
// Deliberately NOT synced: Ramp duration / start delay stay in minutes. A
// wind-down is wall-clock — how long until you are asleep, not a musical
// length — and the cutoffs are pitch, never tempo.
slider11:0<0,1,1{Seconds,Beats}>Drift period unit
slider12:0<0,2,1{Sine,Triangle,Random}>Drift shape
// Drift Play/Rest. The drift wanders for `play` periods, then FREEZES where
// it stands for `rest` periods, then wanders on. Without this the drift never
// settles — it is always moving, and stillness is only reachable by switching
// it off. Counted in drift periods (slider10), so there is no second unit and
// no arithmetic: you set the period once, then say how many of them.
// PER TARGET, like everything else behind the selector — so Left cutoff can
// wander while Right cutoff holds, which makes the WIDTH itself move in steps
// rather than continuously. Both must be > 0 or the gate is off (the default),
// which is bit-identical to the old always-drifting behaviour.
slider13:0<0,64,0.25>Drift play for (periods, 0 = always)
slider14:0<0,64,0.25>Drift rest for (periods, 0 = always)

// --- Ramp (nested selector; signed delta) ---
slider15:0<0,3,1{Left cutoff,Right cutoff,Left resonance,Right resonance}>Ramp target
slider16:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Ramp by (units match target)
slider17:0<0,60,0.1>Ramp duration (minutes)
// Ramp Play/Rest — the STAIRCASE. The ramp advances for `play` minutes, holds
// still for `rest` minutes, and repeats: descend, settle, descend, settle.
// The rests come OUT of the duration, they do NOT extend it — Ramp duration
// still means "you arrive in this many minutes", and the staircase only
// changes HOW you get there, never WHEN. (The alternative would silently make
// a 10-minute ramp take fifteen, which is the unit-lie this suite refuses.)
// Note this is not the same as Ramp engage, which is the MANUAL version of
// the same idea: engage Off freezes the ramp where it stands and On resumes.
// Both must be > 0 or the gate is off (the default) and the ramp is a smooth
// slide exactly as before.
slider18:0<0,60,0.1>Ramp play for (minutes, 0 = smooth)
slider19:0<0,60,0.1>Ramp rest for (minutes, 0 = smooth)
slider20:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>Ramp engage
slider21:0<0,60,0.1>Ramp start delay (minutes)

@init
// NO ext_noinit: REAPER re-runs @init on every transport play / render
// start, which resets all RUNTIME state (filter memory, drift phases,
// ramp clocks). CONFIG banks are guarded by drift_cfg_inited so those
// re-runs do NOT wipe the user's drift/ramp setup (Path A render fix).
// @serialize restores CONFIG across project save/load.

N_TARGETS = 4;   // Left cutoff, Right cutoff, Left resonance, Right resonance

// Chamberlin SVF state: up to 4 cascaded stages per channel, 2 vars
// (low, band) each. L uses slots 0-7, R uses slots 8-15. Reset every
// @init (fresh filter start on play — normal).
// Two state words per stage, per channel, plus the per-stage damping table.
MAXSTAGES = 6;                 // 6 x 2 poles = 72 dB/oct
// Total peak emphasis, in dB, that Resonance = 1 asks for. Spread across the
// stages, so it means the same thing at -12 as at -72.
// 34 nominal, not a rounder 24, because the retired Kellett core reached ~+29 dB
// of real peak at Resonance 0.98 and migrated projects need to be representable.
// Nominal because a Butterworth section delivers a few dB less than it is asked
// for -- 34 here is about +31 dB of actual peak at Resonance 1.
RES_DB_MAX = 34;
sv_L   = 0;                    // 0 .. 2*MAXSTAGES-1
sv_R   = 2*MAXSTAGES;          // and the same again
inv_qb = 4*MAXSTAGES;          // 1/Q per stage, Butterworth, set in @slider
memset(0, 0, 5*MAXSTAGES);

// Per-target banks (16-slot-spaced for growth), based at 4096.
speed_ramp_by_mem            = 4096;   // signed `by` per target (CONFIG)
speed_ramp_dur_mem           = 4112;   // duration minutes per target (CONFIG)
speed_ramp_delay_mem         = 4128;   // start-delay minutes per target (CONFIG)
speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem        = 4144;   // ramp progress 0..1 per target (RUNTIME)
speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem = 4160;   // delay sample counter per target (RUNTIME)
drift_up_mem                 = 4176;   // CONFIG
drift_down_mem               = 4192;   // CONFIG
drift_per_mem                = 4208;   // CONFIG (period in seconds)
drift_shape_mem              = 4224;   // CONFIG
drift_phase_mem              = 4240;   // RUNTIME
drift_prev_mem               = 4256;   // RUNTIME (Random shape)
drift_curr_mem               = 4272;   // RUNTIME (Random shape)
drift_offset_mem             = 4288;   // RUNTIME (computed Hz offset per target)
// Drift Play/Rest, per target. Counted in drift PERIODS.
drift_play_mem               = 4304;   // CONFIG (periods; 0 = gate off)
drift_rest_mem               = 4320;   // CONFIG (periods; 0 = gate off)
drift_pr_resting_mem         = 4336;   // RUNTIME (1 = frozen)
drift_pr_count_mem           = 4352;   // RUNTIME (whole periods in this state)
drift_pr_accum_mem           = 4368;   // RUNTIME (fractional period while frozen)
// Ramp Play/Rest -- the staircase. Counted in MINUTES, matching Ramp duration.
sr_play_mem                  = 4384;   // CONFIG (minutes; 0 = gate off)
sr_rest_mem                  = 4400;   // CONFIG (minutes; 0 = gate off)
sr_pr_resting_mem            = 4416;   // RUNTIME (1 = holding)
sr_pr_accum_mem              = 4432;   // RUNTIME (samples into this window)

// CONFIG defaults — one-time only, so transport re-runs of @init don't
// wipe user setup. @serialize overwrites these on project reload.
drift_cfg_inited != 1 ? (
  i = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    speed_ramp_by_mem[i]    = 0;
    speed_ramp_dur_mem[i]   = 0;
    speed_ramp_delay_mem[i] = 0;
    drift_up_mem[i]         = 0;
    drift_down_mem[i]       = 0;
    drift_per_mem[i]        = 20;
    drift_shape_mem[i]      = 0;
    drift_play_mem[i]       = 0;
    drift_rest_mem[i]       = 0;
    sr_play_mem[i]          = 0;
    sr_rest_mem[i]          = 0;
    i += 1;
  );
  drift_cfg_inited = 1;
);

// RUNTIME reset on EVERY @init (i.e. every transport play). Phases seeded
// with rand so the two drifts don't crest together.
i = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i]        = 0;
  speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] = 0;
  drift_phase_mem[i]  = rand(1);
  drift_prev_mem[i]   = 0;
  drift_curr_mem[i]   = 0;
  drift_offset_mem[i] = 0;
  drift_pr_resting_mem[i] = 0;
  drift_pr_count_mem[i]   = 0;
  drift_pr_accum_mem[i]   = 0;
  sr_pr_resting_mem[i]    = 0;
  sr_pr_accum_mem[i]      = 0;
  i += 1;
);

// Host tempo. ABSOLUTE, never a ratio against a remembered reference — @init
// re-runs on every transport play and would wipe the reference. Seeded here
// so the first @sample after a play has a sane value before @block runs.
host_bpm = max(tempo, 0.001);

@block
// Project tempo, refreshed once per block (it never changes mid-block).
host_bpm = max(tempo, 0.001);

@slider
n_stages = min(MAXSTAGES, slider5 + 1);   // 1..6 cascaded 2-pole stages
// --- Per-stage Q, Butterworth.
// Cascading N IDENTICAL sections is the obvious way to get a steeper slope and
// it is wrong twice over: the resonant peaks multiply (six sections at 6 dB
// each is a 36 dB lump), and the composite -3 dB point slides DOWN by
// sqrt(2^(1/N)-1) -- at six stages a cutoff set to 480 Hz actually corners near
// 168. The number on the slider would drift further from the truth the steeper
// you went, which is the exact dishonesty this suite keeps refusing elsewhere.
//
// A Butterworth cascade gives each section its OWN Q, which is what makes the
// composite maximally flat and puts the corner exactly on the number at every
// slope. For 2N poles, section k takes Q = 1/(2*cos(pi*(2k+1)/(4N))) -- the
// standard pole-angle formula, so no table to keep in step with anything.
// (Butterworth, 1930. The idea is textbook; nothing here is ported.)
vq_order = 2 * n_stages;
vq_s = 0;
loop(n_stages,
  inv_qb[vq_s] = 2 * cos($pi * (2*vq_s + 1) / (2*vq_order));   // = 1/Q directly
  vq_s += 1;
);
// Resonance is spread ACROSS the stages rather than applied to each: the peak
// is the SUM of the sections' peaks in dB, so a given Resonance asks for the
// same total emphasis whether it is one stage or six. Without this, turning
// Resonance up at -72 would be a wall rather than a peak.
res_k = RES_DB_MAX / n_stages * (log(10) / 20);
out_gain = 10 ^ (slider6 / 20);
// (qL/qR are computed per-sample in @sample now — resonance drifts/ramps too.)

// Speed Ramp target selector save/restore. slider15 = target; slider16 =
// per-target `by`; slider17 = per-target duration; slider21 = per-target
// start delay. slider20 (engage) stays GLOBAL.
sel_speed_target = slider15;
last_speed_target != sel_speed_target ? (
  speed_ramp_by_mem[last_speed_target]    = slider16;
  speed_ramp_dur_mem[last_speed_target]   = slider17;
  speed_ramp_delay_mem[last_speed_target] = slider21;
  sr_play_mem[last_speed_target]          = slider18;
  sr_rest_mem[last_speed_target]          = slider19;
  slider16 = speed_ramp_by_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider17 = speed_ramp_dur_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider21 = speed_ramp_delay_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider18 = sr_play_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider19 = sr_rest_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider_automate(slider16);
  slider_automate(slider17);
  slider_automate(slider21);
  slider_automate(slider18);
  slider_automate(slider19);
  last_speed_target = sel_speed_target;
) : (
  speed_ramp_by_mem[sel_speed_target]    = slider16;
  speed_ramp_dur_mem[sel_speed_target]   = slider17;
  speed_ramp_delay_mem[sel_speed_target] = slider21;
  sr_play_mem[sel_speed_target]          = slider18;
  sr_rest_mem[sel_speed_target]          = slider19;
);

// Drift target selector save/restore (same nested-selector pattern).
sel_target = slider7;
last_target_select != sel_target ? (
  drift_up_mem[last_target_select]    = slider8;
  drift_down_mem[last_target_select]  = slider9;
  drift_per_mem[last_target_select]   = slider10;
  drift_shape_mem[last_target_select] = slider12;
  drift_play_mem[last_target_select]  = slider13;
  drift_rest_mem[last_target_select]  = slider14;
  slider8  = drift_up_mem[sel_target];
  slider9  = drift_down_mem[sel_target];
  slider10 = drift_per_mem[sel_target];
  slider12 = drift_shape_mem[sel_target];
  slider13 = drift_play_mem[sel_target];
  slider14 = drift_rest_mem[sel_target];
  slider_automate(slider8);
  slider_automate(slider9);
  slider_automate(slider10);
  slider_automate(slider12);
  slider_automate(slider13);
  slider_automate(slider14);
  last_target_select = sel_target;
) : (
  drift_up_mem[sel_target]    = slider8;
  drift_down_mem[sel_target]  = slider9;
  drift_per_mem[sel_target]   = slider10;
  drift_shape_mem[sel_target] = slider12;
  drift_play_mem[sel_target]  = slider13;
  drift_rest_mem[sel_target]  = slider14;
);

@sample
// mono-sum the source (handles dual-mono or single-sided mono cleanly)
mono_in = (spl0 + spl1) * 0.5;

// --- Advance each target's own ramp clock. Engage (slider20) is a
//     freeze/resume gate: while Off, ramp_t holds; only transport play
//     (via @init) resets it. Each target waits its own start-delay, then
//     advances its own ramp_t 0->1 over its own duration. ---
slider20 ? (
  i = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    speed_ramp_dur_mem[i] > 0 ? (
      sr_delay_samples = speed_ramp_delay_mem[i] * 60 * srate;
      speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] < sr_delay_samples ? (
        speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] += 1;
      ) : (
        // Staircase (sliders 18/19). The ramp advances for `play` minutes,
        // holds for `rest`, and repeats. The rests come OUT of the duration:
        // the advancing step is scaled by (play+rest)/play, so the ramp still
        // completes in exactly `duration` minutes of wall clock. Ramp duration
        // keeps meaning "you arrive in this long" -- the staircase changes
        // only HOW you get there. Off unless BOTH are > 0.
        sr_pl = sr_play_mem[i];
        sr_rs = sr_rest_mem[i];
        sr_pr_on = sr_pl > 0 && sr_rs > 0;
        sr_pr_on ? (
          sr_win = sr_pr_resting_mem[i] ? sr_rs : sr_pl;
          sr_pr_accum_mem[i] += 1;
          sr_pr_accum_mem[i] >= sr_win * 60 * srate ? (
            sr_pr_accum_mem[i] = 0;
            sr_pr_resting_mem[i] = 1 - sr_pr_resting_mem[i];
          );
        );
        // Advance only while not holding. One-armed on purpose -- an empty
        // else-branch is a silent compile failure in eel2 (CLAUDE.md).
        !(sr_pr_on && sr_pr_resting_mem[i]) ? (
          sr_step = 1.0 / (srate * speed_ramp_dur_mem[i] * 60);
          sr_pr_on ? sr_step *= (sr_pl + sr_rs) / sr_pl;
          speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i] += sr_step;
          speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i] > 1.0 ? speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i] = 1.0;
        );
      );
    );
    i += 1;
  );
);
// (ramp offset per target = ramp_t * by; computed inline below with the
//  effective values, all 4 targets in parallel.)

// --- Per-target drift wave. Each target's phase advances per sample at
//     1 / (period_seconds * srate); offset (Hz) = shape * (up or down). ---
i = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  up    = drift_up_mem[i];
  down  = drift_down_mem[i];
  per   = drift_per_mem[i];
  shape = drift_shape_mem[i];

  drift_offset_local = 0;
  (up > 0 || down > 0) && per > 0 ? (
    // Drift period unit (slider11): Seconds = per as-is; Beats = per beats
    // converted at the live project tempo, so the wander follows the host.
    per_seconds = slider11 == 1 ? per * 60 / host_bpm : per;
    period_samples = max(per_seconds * srate, 1);

    // Drift Play/Rest (sliders 13/14), per target, counted in PERIODS.
    // While resting the phase does not advance at all, so shape_val below
    // is recomputed from the held phase and the offset simply STAYS where
    // it stopped -- freeze in place, not a return to centre. The rest
    // interval needs its own clock precisely because the phase is frozen.
    // Off unless BOTH are > 0, which is the default and is bit-identical
    // to the old always-wandering behaviour.
    d_pl = drift_play_mem[i];
    d_rs = drift_rest_mem[i];
    d_pr_on = d_pl > 0 && d_rs > 0;

    // ONE accumulator counting fractional periods in whichever state we are
    // in, ticked every sample whether frozen or not. Two reasons it is not
    // a whole-period counter checked on the phase wrap: that can only ever
    // switch state ON a boundary, and phase 0 is the zero crossing of both
    // Sine and Triangle -- so every freeze landed at NO drift, which is the
    // least interesting point in the cycle and the exact opposite of the
    // point. Fractional values are therefore meaningful, and the FRACTION
    // chooses where the parameter parks: x.25 freezes at the crest (drifted
    // fully up), x.75 at the trough (fully down), x.0 and x.5 at neutral.
    // Verified by simulating the loop, not by reading it -- an earlier
    // version of this comment claimed 2.5 was the crest, and 2.5 is a zero
    // crossing. (Random shape was the one that already worked, since at
    // phase 0 it reads the previous random target rather than zero.)
    d_pr_on ? (
      drift_pr_accum_mem[i] += 1.0 / period_samples;
      drift_pr_accum_mem[i] >= (drift_pr_resting_mem[i] ? d_rs : d_pl) ? (
        drift_pr_accum_mem[i]   = 0;
        drift_pr_resting_mem[i] = 1 - drift_pr_resting_mem[i];
      );
    );

    d_pr_on && drift_pr_resting_mem[i] ? (
      new_phase = drift_phase_mem[i];   // FROZEN: hold wherever we stopped
    ) : (
      new_phase = drift_phase_mem[i] + 1.0 / period_samples;
      new_phase >= 1.0 ? (
        new_phase -= 1.0;
        shape == 2 ? (
          drift_prev_mem[i] = drift_curr_mem[i];
          drift_curr_mem[i] = rand(2) - 1;
        );
      );
      drift_phase_mem[i] = new_phase;
    );

    shape == 0 ? (
      shape_val = sin(2 * $pi * new_phase);
    ) : shape == 1 ? (
      shape_val = new_phase < 0.25 ? (4 * new_phase) :
                  new_phase < 0.75 ? (2 - 4 * new_phase) :
                                     (4 * new_phase - 4);
    ) : (
      shape_val = drift_prev_mem[i] +
        (drift_curr_mem[i] - drift_prev_mem[i]) * new_phase;
    );
    drift_offset_local = shape_val > 0 ? shape_val * up : shape_val * down;
  );
  drift_offset_mem[i] = drift_offset_local;
  i += 1;
);

// --- Effective params = baseline + drift + ramp, per sample. Targets:
//     0 = L cutoff (Hz), 1 = R cutoff (Hz), 2 = L resonance (0-1),
//     3 = R resonance (0-1). Cutoffs clamped to a safe range and turned
//     into SVF f coefficients; resonances clamped 0-1 and turned into
//     SVF q damping (q = 2 - 1.9*res). All recomputed per sample so
//     drift/ramp actually move. ---
ramp0 = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[0] * speed_ramp_by_mem[0];
ramp1 = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[1] * speed_ramp_by_mem[1];
ramp2 = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[2] * speed_ramp_by_mem[2];
ramp3 = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[3] * speed_ramp_by_mem[3];

eff_l_cut = max(20, min(slider1 + drift_offset_mem[0] + ramp0, srate * 0.45));
eff_r_cut = max(20, min(slider2 + drift_offset_mem[1] + ramp1, srate * 0.45));
eff_l_res = max(0,  min(slider3 + drift_offset_mem[2] + ramp2, 1));
eff_r_res = max(0,  min(slider4 + drift_offset_mem[3] + ramp3, 1));

gL = tan($pi * min(eff_l_cut, srate*0.49) / srate);
gR = tan($pi * min(eff_r_cut, srate*0.49) / srate);
// Resonance -> a Q multiplier shared by every stage (see res_k in @slider).
// exp() rather than pow() because only the exponent varies per sample.
inv_qm_L = exp(-res_k * eff_l_res);
inv_qm_R = exp(-res_k * eff_r_res);

// --- Left chain: n_stages lowpass stages in series ---
// Topology-preserving (zero-delay-feedback) SVF, one 2-pole lowpass per stage.
// The old Chamberlin form goes unstable at high Q near Nyquist, which a
// six-stage Butterworth cascade reaches on its own -- its last section sits at
// Q 3.83 before Resonance touches it. This form is stable across the range.
in_L = mono_in;
s = 0;
loop(n_stages,
  idx = s * 2;
  kk  = inv_qb[s] * inv_qm_L;
  a1  = 1 / (1 + gL*(gL + kk));
  a2  = gL * a1;
  a3  = gL * a2;
  v3  = in_L - sv_L[idx + 1];
  v1  = a1*sv_L[idx] + a2*v3;
  v2  = sv_L[idx + 1] + a2*sv_L[idx] + a3*v3;
  sv_L[idx]     = 2*v1 - sv_L[idx];
  sv_L[idx + 1] = 2*v2 - sv_L[idx + 1];
  in_L = v2;                     // v2 is the lowpass output
  s += 1;
);

// --- Right chain: independent cutoff + resonance, shared slope ---
in_R = mono_in;
s = 0;
loop(n_stages,
  idx = s * 2;
  kk  = inv_qb[s] * inv_qm_R;
  a1  = 1 / (1 + gR*(gR + kk));
  a2  = gR * a1;
  a3  = gR * a2;
  v3  = in_R - sv_R[idx + 1];
  v1  = a1*sv_R[idx] + a2*v3;
  v2  = sv_R[idx + 1] + a2*sv_R[idx] + a3*v3;
  sv_R[idx]     = 2*v1 - sv_R[idx];
  sv_R[idx + 1] = 2*v2 - sv_R[idx + 1];
  in_R = v2;
  s += 1;
);

spl0 = in_L * out_gain;
spl1 = in_R * out_gain;

@serialize
// Persist per-target Speed Ramp (by / duration / start-delay) + drift
// configs so reload restores BOTH targets, not just whichever was selected
// at save. Version-guarded (CLAUDE.md @serialize gotcha): magic encodes the
// target count so a format change degrades to @init defaults, not garbage.
wv_magic = 3200000 + N_TARGETS;
file_var(0, wv_magic);
wv_magic == 3200000 + N_TARGETS ? (
  file_mem(0, speed_ramp_by_mem,    N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, speed_ramp_dur_mem,   N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, speed_ramp_delay_mem, N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, sr_play_mem,          N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, sr_rest_mem,          N_TARGETS);
  file_var(0, last_speed_target);
  file_mem(0, drift_up_mem,    N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, drift_down_mem,  N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, drift_per_mem,   N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, drift_shape_mem, N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, drift_play_mem,  N_TARGETS);
  file_mem(0, drift_rest_mem,  N_TARGETS);
  file_var(0, last_target_select);

  // TRACK-DUPLICATE FIX. On READ (load/duplicate) file_avail(0) >= 0; on
  // WRITE it is < 0. @serialize is the only section guaranteed to run on
  // both load AND duplicate, so it re-establishes the bank<->slider
  // invariant: force the visible config sliders to match the restored
  // banks for the restored selectors, correcting any transient @slider
  // clobber. (sliderchange, NOT slider_automate — no automation write.)
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
    slider16 = speed_ramp_by_mem[last_speed_target];
    slider17 = speed_ramp_dur_mem[last_speed_target];
    slider21 = speed_ramp_delay_mem[last_speed_target];
    slider18 = sr_play_mem[last_speed_target];
    slider19 = sr_rest_mem[last_speed_target];
    slider8  = drift_up_mem[last_target_select];
    slider9  = drift_down_mem[last_target_select];
    slider10 = drift_per_mem[last_target_select];
    slider12 = drift_shape_mem[last_target_select];
    slider13 = drift_play_mem[last_target_select];
    slider14 = drift_rest_mem[last_target_select];
    sliderchange(-1);
  );
);
